.TH expr 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
expr \- evaluate an expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expr
.I "argument ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B expr
evaluates the expression made from its arguments and writes the result
to standard output.  Every operator and operand must be a separate
argument, so the shell has to be kept from eating them:
.B expr 3 \e* 4
and not
.BR "expr 3*4" .
.SH OPERATORS
In increasing order of precedence.
.TP
.IB e1 " | " e2
.I e1
if it is neither empty nor zero, otherwise
.IR e2 .
.TP
.IB e1 " & " e2
.I e1
if
.I e2
is neither empty nor zero, otherwise 0.
.TP
.B < > <= >= == !=
Compare, giving 1 or 0.  Numeric if both sides are numbers, otherwise
by string.
.TP
.B + \-
Add, subtract.
.TP
.B * / %
Multiply, divide, remainder.
.TP
.IB e1 " : " e2
Match the string
.I e1
against the regular expression
.IR e2 ,
anchored at the start.  The result is the number of characters matched,
or the text of the first
.BR \\( ... \\)
group if there is one.
.TP
.BI len " e"
The length of
.IR e .
.TP
.BI ! " e"
1 if
.I e
is empty or zero, otherwise 0.
.TP
.BI \- " e"
Negate a number.
.TP
.BI ( " e" " )"
Grouping.
.TP
.BI { " c" " , " t " }"
.I t
if
.I c
is true, otherwise 0.
.TP
.BI { " c" " , " t " , " f " }"
.I t
if
.I c
is true, otherwise
.IR f .
This conditional is an extension; other systems do not have it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if the result is neither empty nor the number zero, 1 if it is, 2 for
a bad expression.
.SH NOTES
Equality is
.B ==
here.  A plain
.B =
is
.I not
an operator and gives a syntax error \- which is the opposite of POSIX
and of every other
.B expr
you will have met.  Watch for it in scripts brought from elsewhere.
.PP
There is no
.BR match ,
.BR substr ,
.B index
or
.B length
keyword.  Use
.B :
for match and
.B len
for length.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# expr 1 + 2
# expr 10 / 3
# expr abcdef : 'a.c'
# expr len abcdef
# n=`expr $n + 1`
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR test (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR dc (1)
